Dear Adult Protective Services,

Thank you for taking the time to review my concerns.

I am a sixty-two-year-old disabled resident of a licensed room-and-board home in San Bernardino County. I am writing because I have experienced an ongoing pattern of interactions with the house manager, Vince, that I believe has had a significant effect on my emotional well-being.

I want to emphasize that I do not want to move. My goal is to remain in my current residence if the situation can be improved. Because I have Asperger’s syndrome and bipolar disorder, major transitions are particularly difficult for me. I have been working to establish routines, friendships, and community connections, and I would like the opportunity to continue living here in a respectful and stable environment.

Over the past several months, I have experienced repeated confrontations that I found intimidating, humiliating, or unnecessarily confrontational. These have included public criticism, questioning about my private medical information, inconsistent enforcement of house rules, and conversations in which I felt I was being singled out. I have attempted to remain calm, comply with instructions, and resolve concerns respectfully.

The continuing stress has affected my well-being. Although I cannot state that it directly caused my medical problems, I believe it contributed to increased stress, reduced sleep, and worsening symptoms related to my bipolar disorder.

I am asking Adult Protective Services to help improve the living situation rather than relocate me. My hope is that reasonable communication, respect for personal boundaries, and consistent treatment of all residents can resolve these problems while allowing me to remain in my current home.

I have attached a chronology of events documenting the incidents described in this letter.

Thank you for your time and consideration.

Sincerely,

Alexander Miller